1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,450
Imagine a steel rod. You push one end.

2
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:06,210
The other end moves immediately, right?

3
00:00:07,028 --> 00:00:10,238
It is steel, it is solid, the
whole rod has to move together...

4
00:00:11,158 --> 00:00:12,358
yes?

5
00:00:12,852 --> 00:00:14,052
Well...

6
00:00:14,429 --> 00:00:15,629
No.

7
00:00:15,991 --> 00:00:17,381
Let's dig into it.

8
00:00:18,863 --> 00:00:20,793
Maybe you already know the answer.

9
00:00:21,583 --> 00:00:23,513
Nothing can move faster than light.

10
00:00:24,401 --> 00:00:27,671
So the other end obviously
can't respond immediately.

11
00:00:28,341 --> 00:00:32,071
Light would need just over three seconds
to travel one million kilometres.

12
00:00:33,545 --> 00:00:35,705
So that's our answer, right? Not quite.

13
00:00:35,785 --> 00:00:38,155
Because the really
interesting question is this.

14
00:00:39,005 --> 00:00:41,595
How fast can a steel rod actually respond?

15
00:00:44,305 --> 00:00:46,715
Even steel isn't perfectly rigid.

16
00:00:47,525 --> 00:00:50,335
When you push one end, you
slightly compress the material.

17
00:00:51,272 --> 00:00:53,222
Those atoms push on their neighbours.

18
00:00:53,952 --> 00:00:55,622
Those push on the next atoms.

19
00:00:56,554 --> 00:00:59,624
So the disturbance travels
through the material as a wave.

20
00:01:00,533 --> 00:01:01,903
And the question is this.

21
00:01:02,353 --> 00:01:04,443
How fast does that wave travel?

22
00:01:05,217 --> 00:01:08,157
Well... steel is actually
pretty good at transmitting it.

23
00:01:08,237 --> 00:01:09,927
But not that good.

24
00:01:10,755 --> 00:01:12,885
About five kilometres per second.

25
00:01:13,575 --> 00:01:15,905
That's roughly the
speed of sound in steel.

26
00:01:16,784 --> 00:01:18,664
Which sounds pretty damn fast.

27
00:01:18,744 --> 00:01:21,164
Until you remember how long our rod is.

28
00:01:21,244 --> 00:01:23,594
One million kilometres.

29
00:01:24,671 --> 00:01:27,771
At five kilometres per second, the
disturbance would need roughly...

30
00:01:27,851 --> 00:01:31,461
fifty-five hours to reach the other end.

31
00:01:32,221 --> 00:01:34,051
You push the rod on Monday morning...

32
00:01:34,201 --> 00:01:38,011
and the other end doesn't start
moving until Wednesday afternoon.

33
00:01:40,941 --> 00:01:44,511
This sounds ridiculous. Steel feels solid.

34
00:01:45,341 --> 00:01:47,061
If you hit a steel bar with a hammer, it

35
00:01:47,141 --> 00:01:49,271
doesn't look like you're
compressing it at all.

36
00:01:50,022 --> 00:01:52,232
So why doesn't the whole
thing move together?

37
00:01:53,105 --> 00:01:54,785
What is actually travelling
through the steel?

38
00:01:54,865 --> 00:01:58,615
To answer that, we have to zoom in. A lot.

39
00:01:59,453 --> 00:02:01,733
A steel rod is, physically, an enormous

40
00:02:01,813 --> 00:02:04,563
collection of atoms
interacting with one another.

41
00:02:05,245 --> 00:02:08,955
When you push one end, you don't somehow
give every atom in the rod an order.

42
00:02:09,745 --> 00:02:10,945
"Move."

43
00:02:11,423 --> 00:02:15,713
The atoms closest to your hand are
pushed first. They move slightly.

44
00:02:16,705 --> 00:02:19,995
The rod doesn't respond as
one perfectly rigid object.

45
00:02:20,885 --> 00:02:23,835
It responds progressively,
from one part to the next.

46
00:02:26,525 --> 00:02:28,415
So let's zoom in even further.

47
00:02:29,105 --> 00:02:30,555
Steel is made of atoms.

48
00:02:31,265 --> 00:02:33,315
And atoms are not tiny steel balls.

49
00:02:34,379 --> 00:02:36,289
They're quantum mechanical systems.

50
00:02:36,719 --> 00:02:40,489
A tiny nucleus sits in the
middle, surrounded by electrons.

51
00:02:41,593 --> 00:02:45,523
And almost all of an atom's volume
is not occupied by the nucleus.

52
00:02:46,489 --> 00:02:49,449
So when you look at a solid
object, you're not looking at a

53
00:02:49,529 --> 00:02:53,119
collection of microscopic solid
balls packed tightly together.

54
00:02:53,859 --> 00:02:58,109
You're looking at an enormous system held
together by interactions between atoms.

55
00:02:59,075 --> 00:03:01,445
So what actually holds
all of this together?

56
00:03:02,195 --> 00:03:04,725
The answer is mostly electromagnetism.

57
00:03:05,509 --> 00:03:10,019
Each positively charged nucleus interacts
with negatively charged electrons.

58
00:03:10,729 --> 00:03:13,879
Electrons also interact
with neighbouring atoms.

59
00:03:14,781 --> 00:03:17,261
And depending on the
material, these interactions

60
00:03:17,341 --> 00:03:19,791
create different kinds
of chemical bonding.

61
00:03:20,679 --> 00:03:23,889
In metals, electrons can
spread out across many atoms.

62
00:03:25,019 --> 00:03:28,649
This is part of what gives metals
their characteristic metallic bonding.

63
00:03:29,524 --> 00:03:31,254
But now we have another problem.

64
00:03:31,924 --> 00:03:33,734
If atoms attract each other...

65
00:03:34,184 --> 00:03:36,134
why don't they simply collapse together?

66
00:03:36,969 --> 00:03:39,539
Imagine two atoms. Move them closer.

67
00:03:40,309 --> 00:03:41,819
At first, there can be attraction.

68
00:03:42,849 --> 00:03:43,889
Move them even closer...

69
00:03:43,969 --> 00:03:46,669
and the energy rises very rapidly.

70
00:03:46,749 --> 00:03:49,699
The electron clouds begin to overlap.

71
00:03:50,569 --> 00:03:52,769
And quantum mechanics prevents identical

72
00:03:52,849 --> 00:03:55,759
electrons from occupying
the same quantum states.

73
00:03:56,489 --> 00:04:00,199
This is where the Pauli exclusion
principle becomes important.

74
00:04:01,163 --> 00:04:02,933
So matter has an equilibrium.

75
00:04:03,523 --> 00:04:07,913
Too far apart, and attractive
interactions can pull atoms together.

76
00:04:08,728 --> 00:04:13,118
Too close, and quantum effects and
electromagnetic repulsion push back.

77
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:15,450
There is a preferred arrangement.

78
00:04:16,220 --> 00:04:20,730
And on a macroscopic scale, we have
a name for the result. Solid matter.

79
00:04:23,570 --> 00:04:26,220
The atoms in a solid
aren't frozen in place.

80
00:04:26,970 --> 00:04:28,440
They're constantly moving.

81
00:04:29,279 --> 00:04:32,229
And when you push on it,
you're disturbing that system.

82
00:04:33,087 --> 00:04:36,537
Now put your hand on a
table. You can feel it.

83
00:04:37,227 --> 00:04:40,837
It feels like your hand has
encountered a completely solid surface.

84
00:04:41,720 --> 00:04:42,920
But zoom in.

85
00:04:43,220 --> 00:04:47,170
There isn't a microscopic wall made
of perfectly touching little balls.

86
00:04:48,111 --> 00:04:51,641
The atoms in your hand interact
with the atoms in the table.

87
00:04:52,453 --> 00:04:54,593
Their electrons interact with each other.

88
00:04:54,673 --> 00:04:59,703
And quantum mechanics prevents the atoms
from simply passing through each other.

89
00:05:00,453 --> 00:05:04,243
The result is a force that stops your
hand from passing through the table.

90
00:05:05,011 --> 00:05:07,891
What you experience as
"touch" is the macroscopic

91
00:05:07,971 --> 00:05:10,601
consequence of all these
microscopic interactions.

92
00:05:11,742 --> 00:05:15,752
So when you put your hand on a table,
what does "touching" actually mean?

93
00:05:16,510 --> 00:05:21,000
It's an interaction. Your hand interacts
with the atoms in the material.

94
00:05:21,770 --> 00:05:23,400
The material pushes back.

95
00:05:25,870 --> 00:05:28,700
And now the original steel
rod makes more sense.

96
00:05:29,610 --> 00:05:33,260
Its response isn't determined
by how "solid" it feels to us.

97
00:05:33,947 --> 00:05:35,927
It's determined by how
quickly a mechanical

98
00:05:36,007 --> 00:05:38,857
disturbance can propagate
through the material.

99
00:05:39,675 --> 00:05:42,465
Could we make that faster? Absolutely.

100
00:05:43,275 --> 00:05:45,985
Different materials have
different sound velocities.

101
00:05:46,815 --> 00:05:49,525
Steel is around five
kilometres per second.

102
00:05:49,975 --> 00:05:53,145
Diamond can exceed ten
kilometres per second.

103
00:05:53,953 --> 00:05:56,023
But none of them are infinitely fast.

104
00:05:56,993 --> 00:06:00,983
And that's not just an engineering
limitation. It's fundamental.

105
00:06:04,063 --> 00:06:07,573
Imagine we somehow invented
the stiffest possible material.

106
00:06:07,763 --> 00:06:10,733
A material that was perfectly rigid.

107
00:06:11,649 --> 00:06:16,679
You push one end... and the other end
responds immediately. Sounds great.

108
00:06:16,949 --> 00:06:18,519
But now we have a problem.

109
00:06:19,331 --> 00:06:23,761
You could use the rod to transmit
information. Push the rod. One.

110
00:06:24,549 --> 00:06:26,059
Don't push it. Zero.

111
00:06:27,013 --> 00:06:29,793
If the other end responded
instantly, you would

112
00:06:29,873 --> 00:06:32,743
have a way to transmit
information instantaneously.

113
00:06:33,594 --> 00:06:35,564
That isn't allowed by relativity.

114
00:06:36,574 --> 00:06:39,884
Physical influences cannot
propagate faster than light.

115
00:06:40,732 --> 00:06:44,282
So a perfectly rigid object
cannot exist in our universe.

116
00:06:45,112 --> 00:06:47,512
Perfect rigidity would
require instantaneous

117
00:06:47,592 --> 00:06:50,762
communication between
different parts of the object.

118
00:06:53,702 --> 00:06:54,982
Remember our imaginary rod?

119
00:06:55,062 --> 00:06:57,802
One million kilometres.

120
00:06:58,362 --> 00:07:01,912
Even light needs just over three
seconds to cross that distance.

121
00:07:02,762 --> 00:07:06,282
So even if we replaced our steel rod
with some completely hypothetical

122
00:07:06,362 --> 00:07:11,112
material that was as close as physically
possible to the ultimate limit...

123
00:07:11,925 --> 00:07:13,375
there would still be a delay.

124
00:07:14,105 --> 00:07:16,695
Nothing can make the
information arrive instantly.

125
00:07:19,615 --> 00:07:21,205
Think about what we've just done.

126
00:07:21,935 --> 00:07:25,925
We started with something
completely ordinary. A steel rod.

127
00:07:26,755 --> 00:07:29,645
Then we asked why the far
end doesn't move immediately.

128
00:07:30,575 --> 00:07:32,385
That led us to mechanical waves.

129
00:07:33,359 --> 00:07:35,449
Mechanical waves led us to atoms.

130
00:07:36,419 --> 00:07:40,889
Atoms led us to electromagnetism.
Then quantum mechanics.

131
00:07:41,865 --> 00:07:44,925
And suddenly we're talking about
the structure of matter itself.

132
00:07:45,005 --> 00:07:47,595
All because someone pushed a stick.

133
00:07:50,355 --> 00:07:53,385
The word "solid" sounds
like a fundamental property.

134
00:07:54,269 --> 00:07:57,649
But "solid" is really a
macroscopic description of

135
00:07:57,729 --> 00:08:00,579
how an enormous number of
particles behave together.

136
00:08:01,341 --> 00:08:05,541
At the microscopic level, matter
is dynamic. Atoms vibrate.

137
00:08:05,621 --> 00:08:08,571
Electrons occupy quantum states.

138
00:08:08,761 --> 00:08:11,611
Fields interact. Energy moves around.

139
00:08:12,473 --> 00:08:15,253
And yet, when billions upon
billions of these interactions

140
00:08:15,333 --> 00:08:19,203
work together, we get something
that feels completely solid.

141
00:08:19,950 --> 00:08:22,440
A table. A rock. A wall. Your hand.

142
00:08:24,810 --> 00:08:27,460
And here's the part I
find most remarkable.

143
00:08:28,430 --> 00:08:32,660
The same physical laws that make the
table solid also make your hand solid.

144
00:08:33,420 --> 00:08:37,490
And those same laws allow your brain
to detect the forces between them.

145
00:08:38,145 --> 00:08:40,325
Your brain receives electrical signals.

146
00:08:40,405 --> 00:08:41,605
It processes them.

147
00:08:41,685 --> 00:08:44,155
And it constructs an experience.

148
00:08:45,005 --> 00:08:47,015
The feeling of touching a solid object.

149
00:08:47,931 --> 00:08:50,011
So the thing experiencing
the table is made

150
00:08:50,091 --> 00:08:52,301
from the same kind of matter as the table.

151
00:08:55,371 --> 00:08:59,181
So the next time you put your hand on a
table, you can think of it differently.

152
00:09:00,096 --> 00:09:02,126
It isn't a perfectly rigid block.

153
00:09:02,876 --> 00:09:04,646
It's an enormous quantum system.

154
00:09:05,527 --> 00:09:09,757
And when you push on it, the disturbance
travels through the material as a wave.

155
00:09:10,594 --> 00:09:12,064
The table feels solid.

156
00:09:12,334 --> 00:09:16,084
But underneath that familiar
solidity is something much stranger.

157
00:09:17,071 --> 00:09:19,841
A universe where almost
nothing is quite what it seems.

158
00:09:20,071 --> 00:09:21,461
And somehow...

159
00:09:21,611 --> 00:09:23,241
it all holds together.
